        "thinking": "Two categories of build errors:\n\n1. `Could not resolve \"@embedded-postgres/darwin-x64\"` etc. — these come from the `embedded-postgres` package being bundled in. This is a Node.js-only package that shouldn't be included in the Workers bundle. It's being pulled in transitively from somewhere in the server code. I need to mark these as external.\n\n2. `Could not resolve \"node:sqlite\"` — from `undici`. Same issue.\n\nThe solution is to add these as external modules in wrangler's config. I can do this with a `build` section in `wrangler.toml` that specifies `external` modules.\n \nLooking at the wrangler config, I need to figure out the right way to specify externals. Wrangler supports esbuild configuration, so I should be able to add an `[esbuild]` section or similar to mark `embedded-postgres`, `@embedded-postgres/*`, and `node:sqlite` as external dependencies that shouldn't be bundled. `--no-bundle` with a pre-built file would work but adds complexity. The real issue is that the server routes are being bundled into the worker, and they transitively pull in `embedded-postgres` through their dependencies. I need to either mark those packages as external in the wrangler config, or trace back through the import chain to understand why they're being included in the first place.\n\nLooking at the wrangler documentation, I'm checking how to properly declare external packages so they don't get bundled. The worker should be using Hyperdrive for the database, not embedded postgres, so I need to find where in the server code this dependency is being pulled in and either exclude it or prevent it from being bundled.",
